1
00:01:42,235 --> 00:01:44,670
Bravo. That was beautiful.

2
00:01:44,838 --> 00:01:46,500
Is that the piece you're
gonna play today?

3
00:01:47,574 --> 00:01:49,270
I don't know. Maybe.

4
00:01:49,443 --> 00:01:52,277
Hey, relax. You know
you've got the talent.

5
00:01:52,446 --> 00:01:55,041
All you gotta do is relax and
it'll come shining through, right?

6
00:01:55,215 --> 00:01:56,444
That's what you always say.

7
00:01:56,617 --> 00:01:59,382
And that's what
always happens, isn't it?

8
00:02:01,288 --> 00:02:02,654
I hate these competitions.

9
00:02:02,823 --> 00:02:03,847
I know.

10
00:02:04,024 --> 00:02:06,926
But you win this one, and you
get to go to the conservatory, right?

11
00:02:07,661 --> 00:02:09,596
You're making such a
big deal out of this, Dad.

12
00:02:09,763 --> 00:02:11,664
Because it's important.

13
00:02:11,832 --> 00:02:14,097
Now, you want it, I know that.

14
00:02:14,267 --> 00:02:15,667
And I want it, bad.

15
00:02:16,303 --> 00:02:18,204
- I know you do.
- Yeah.

16
00:02:18,372 --> 00:02:20,364
It's late. Time
for you to change.

17
00:02:20,540 --> 00:02:22,338
Change?

18
00:02:22,509 --> 00:02:23,772
Why can't I wear this?

19
00:02:23,944 --> 00:02:26,140
The judging starts the
moment you set foot onstage,

20
00:02:26,313 --> 00:02:28,433
and you're not setting foot
onstage dressed like that.

21
00:02:28,582 --> 00:02:30,142
Now, young lady, go
upstairs and change.

22
00:02:30,283 --> 00:02:31,911
All right, Dad.

23
00:02:32,085 --> 00:02:35,055
If it'll get you off my back.

24
00:03:01,682 --> 00:03:03,242
Mrs. Baldwin just
brought the judges in.

25
00:03:03,383 --> 00:03:04,874
- How do they look?
- Serious.

26
00:03:05,052 --> 00:03:06,315
Oh.

27
00:03:06,486 --> 00:03:09,979
Juliet, you'll do
fine. You're good.

28
00:03:13,427 --> 00:03:16,761
Okay. Well, it's time.

29
00:03:16,930 --> 00:03:19,090
Before I give you the order
in which you'll be playing,

30
00:03:19,232 --> 00:03:24,330
first let me say that Sandra and I
have had all of you in our classes,

31
00:03:24,504 --> 00:03:26,837
and we think you're
all extremely talented.

32
00:03:27,007 --> 00:03:30,842
And no matter what happens
today, there are no losers among you.

33
00:03:31,011 --> 00:03:32,741
Good luck.

34
00:03:34,281 --> 00:03:35,977
Okay.

35
00:03:36,383 --> 00:03:40,184
Darling, if you're looking for the
list, I saw it on your desk at home.

36
00:03:40,821 --> 00:03:43,814
Well, all right. First up
will be Karen Stryker.

37
00:03:43,990 --> 00:03:48,485
Second, Laura. Third,
Becky. Fourth, Peter.

38
00:03:48,662 --> 00:03:52,155
Fifth, Juliet. Uh,
sixth, Samantha.

39
00:04:42,215 --> 00:04:44,650
Again, that was Juliet DeLong.

40
00:04:49,723 --> 00:04:51,851
That was beautiful.

41
00:04:52,025 --> 00:04:53,755
You nailed it. You
absolutely nailed it.

42
00:04:53,927 --> 00:04:55,122
Nice going, Juliet.

43
00:04:55,295 --> 00:04:57,696
Old Frederic himself
couldn't have played it better.

44
00:05:03,570 --> 00:05:06,404
And the winner of
today's semi-final round

45
00:05:06,573 --> 00:05:11,773
of the Atlanta Conservatory
Chopin Competition is:

46
00:05:13,914 --> 00:05:15,507
Miss Rebecca Smith.

47
00:06:08,368 --> 00:06:10,269
Juliet!

48
00:06:15,108 --> 00:06:16,804
No, baby.

49
00:06:17,978 --> 00:06:20,846
No. No.

50
00:06:21,014 --> 00:06:23,313
No!

51
00:06:46,873 --> 00:06:48,637
Oh, Ben. Ben, it's
good to see you.

52
00:06:48,808 --> 00:06:51,744
Oh, Frank, I just stopped by to
say how sorry I am about Juliet.

53
00:06:51,912 --> 00:06:54,905
- I know how much she meant to you.
- Oh, well, thank you.

54
00:06:55,081 --> 00:06:58,449
Yeah. If there's
anything I can do...

55
00:06:59,052 --> 00:07:01,920
Ben, would you come with
me for a minute, please?

56
00:07:14,634 --> 00:07:16,694
Ben, I don't believe
that Juliet killed herself.

57
00:07:21,808 --> 00:07:24,334
I pushed her too hard, Ben.

58
00:07:24,744 --> 00:07:26,679
I know that.

59
00:07:28,114 --> 00:07:30,549
But she was my daughter.

60
00:07:30,784 --> 00:07:32,650
Her doing well meant
everything to me.

61
00:07:32,819 --> 00:07:36,187
She had to win that competition
because she needed the scholarship

62
00:07:36,356 --> 00:07:38,416
because I couldn't afford...

63
00:07:40,460 --> 00:07:44,261
She was under enough pressure that
anyone might have killed themselves,

64
00:07:44,431 --> 00:07:46,195
but somehow I know
she couldn't do that.

65
00:07:49,602 --> 00:07:51,195
Um...

66
00:07:51,538 --> 00:07:55,339
Frank, I, I know it's, it's
hard for people to accept...

67
00:07:55,508 --> 00:07:58,171
When Carol was dying of
cancer, Ben, I faced that,

68
00:07:58,345 --> 00:08:02,043
and when the hospital bills
left me bankrupt, I faced that.

69
00:08:02,215 --> 00:08:07,017
But I will not accept that
Juliet tried to kill herself.

70
00:08:08,054 --> 00:08:09,454
Help me.

71
00:08:11,091 --> 00:08:13,993
- What?
- Help me to see.

72
00:08:14,794 --> 00:08:16,922
Maybe she didn't.

73
00:08:17,464 --> 00:08:18,727
Will you help me?

74
00:08:39,252 --> 00:08:43,587
You know that girl, Juliet, that
killed herself, the piano player?

75
00:08:43,757 --> 00:08:44,781
Yes.

76
00:08:44,958 --> 00:08:48,520
Well, I know her father,
and he doesn't think she did.

77
00:08:48,695 --> 00:08:55,465
And he wonders... He said he...
He said he could use our help.

78
00:08:56,136 --> 00:08:57,160
Our help?

79
00:08:57,337 --> 00:09:00,705
Well, we could go down,
you know, to the conservatory,

80
00:09:00,874 --> 00:09:02,775
look around and
ask a few questions.

81
00:09:02,942 --> 00:09:04,467
We?

82
00:09:09,983 --> 00:09:11,110
Company.

83
00:09:16,756 --> 00:09:20,352
Oh, hello. Dad, look who's here.

84
00:09:21,528 --> 00:09:23,360
Hello, Ben.

85
00:09:23,663 --> 00:09:25,131
Billy.

86
00:09:26,066 --> 00:09:27,864
Come on in.

87
00:09:36,242 --> 00:09:38,006
Well, ain't you gonna
invite me to sit down?

88
00:09:41,481 --> 00:09:43,006
Billy, sit down.

89
00:09:47,020 --> 00:09:48,989
Well, ain't you gonna offer
me some refreshments?

90
00:09:50,356 --> 00:09:53,952
- There's popcorn.
- How about some herbal tea?

91
00:09:54,794 --> 00:09:58,060
Thank you, Leanne.
You're a nice person.

92
00:10:00,467 --> 00:10:04,734
Well, I got some good news.
My brother's coming to town.

93
00:10:05,171 --> 00:10:08,733
- Russ?
- You remembered his name?

94
00:10:09,008 --> 00:10:10,271
He said you probably wouldn't.

95
00:10:10,443 --> 00:10:12,935
Well, sure, who
could forget Russ?

96
00:10:13,113 --> 00:10:15,673
Russ. "Russ the Rhino"
we used to call him.

97
00:10:15,849 --> 00:10:19,081
Not because he was
big, because he was fast.

98
00:10:19,419 --> 00:10:22,412
Russ. He used to
love to have fun.

99
00:10:22,589 --> 00:10:25,582
Fun, fun. Oh, Russ.

100
00:10:25,758 --> 00:10:28,227
Be glad to see him. Russ.

101
00:10:28,828 --> 00:10:31,297
Russ.

102
00:10:31,831 --> 00:10:34,460
He doesn't know Leanne.
Make sure you invite her.

103
00:10:35,335 --> 00:10:36,394
Invite her to what?

104
00:10:36,569 --> 00:10:38,902
To the dinner party you're
throwing for him tomorrow night.

105
00:10:39,072 --> 00:10:41,701
He's your brother. Why don't
you throw him a dinner party?

106
00:10:41,875 --> 00:10:44,606
I don't have any
dining-room table.

107
00:10:45,545 --> 00:10:47,343
Lucy took it when
she moved to Florida.

108
00:10:50,450 --> 00:10:52,490
You're the one that left
her at the altar like that.

109
00:10:52,619 --> 00:10:55,521
She didn't move in with Russ, no,
didn't cause him 40 years of misery.

110
00:10:55,688 --> 00:10:56,951
She moved in with me.

111
00:10:57,123 --> 00:10:59,285
I'll tell you what, Billy.

112
00:10:59,659 --> 00:11:01,719
I'll throw him a dinner party.

113
00:11:01,895 --> 00:11:04,524
Here. Tomorrow night, 6:30.

114
00:11:04,697 --> 00:11:06,893
You, me, Leanne, Russ.

115
00:11:07,066 --> 00:11:09,535
- How about it? Okay?
- Okay.

116
00:11:12,205 --> 00:11:13,468
Billy.

117
00:11:14,440 --> 00:11:17,433
On the way out the door,
don't call me a jackass.

118
00:11:22,982 --> 00:11:24,644
Jackass.

119
00:11:31,391 --> 00:11:33,189
Fun.

120
00:11:53,112 --> 00:11:57,174
Can you think of anybody that
disliked Juliet or had it in for her?

121
00:11:57,350 --> 00:12:00,752
The only enemy Juliet had
around here was herself.

122
00:12:00,920 --> 00:12:02,946
Not herself, her father.

123
00:12:03,423 --> 00:12:05,517
He pushed her very hard.

124
00:12:05,692 --> 00:12:09,026
Sometimes it seemed she played
the piano for him, not for herself.

125
00:12:09,195 --> 00:12:11,187
Sounds like she was
not a very happy person.

126
00:12:11,864 --> 00:12:14,163
She sure wasn't
after the competition.

127
00:12:20,873 --> 00:12:22,136
Anything?

128
00:12:22,308 --> 00:12:23,867
Frank, uh...

129
00:12:24,077 --> 00:12:29,880
My daughter talked to a lot
of people at the conservatory,

130
00:12:30,483 --> 00:12:32,384
and she found nothing.

131
00:12:32,852 --> 00:12:34,445
Nothing?

132
00:12:34,887 --> 00:12:38,654
I don't think anybody
killed your daughter.

133
00:12:38,925 --> 00:12:41,394
Very few people knew her.
Music was her whole life.

134
00:12:41,561 --> 00:12:43,757
And you said yourself,
you pushed her too hard.

135
00:12:44,597 --> 00:12:46,190
Yes, I know.

136
00:12:49,035 --> 00:12:50,367
Can you let it go?

137
00:12:51,170 --> 00:12:54,106
No, I can't do that. I'm
sorry, Ben. I just can't.

138
00:12:54,274 --> 00:12:59,042
Well, the police found traces
of barbiturates in her body.

139
00:12:59,212 --> 00:13:01,977
That's common in suicides.

140
00:13:02,148 --> 00:13:04,174
Will you look in the
garage, Ben, at her car?

141
00:13:05,351 --> 00:13:08,985
Frank... Ben, please.

142
00:13:28,641 --> 00:13:31,133
<i>Certainly you're entitled
to your own opinion.</i>

143
00:13:41,454 --> 00:13:43,047
Ben?

144
00:13:49,095 --> 00:13:53,692
There's a phone call for you,
Ben, somebody named Billy.

145
00:13:55,501 --> 00:13:58,096
Did you find anything?

146
00:13:59,472 --> 00:14:00,940
Battery's not dead.

147
00:14:01,607 --> 00:14:06,511
This is an older car
with an old battery.

148
00:14:06,913 --> 00:14:10,281
If this car had run
out of gas here,

149
00:14:10,683 --> 00:14:14,120
and the key had
been on for four hours,

150
00:14:14,287 --> 00:14:16,256
the battery would be dead.

151
00:14:20,393 --> 00:14:23,090
I don't think your
daughter died in this car.

152
00:14:26,165 --> 00:14:29,863
I don't even think she
died in your garage.

153
00:15:04,337 --> 00:15:07,000
Hi. Me again. Leanne Mclntyre.

154
00:15:07,173 --> 00:15:08,539
Hi.

155
00:15:08,708 --> 00:15:09,732
That was wonderful.

156
00:15:09,909 --> 00:15:13,346
The "Minute Waltz,"
right? One of my favorites.

157
00:15:13,513 --> 00:15:15,812
It's practically
everybody's favorite.

158
00:15:17,183 --> 00:15:19,914
Mind if I ask you one
or two more questions?

159
00:15:20,620 --> 00:15:22,111
Sure.

160
00:15:22,288 --> 00:15:26,817
I understand you were the closest
thing Juliet had to a best friend.

161
00:15:26,993 --> 00:15:28,791
That's not saying much.

162
00:15:28,961 --> 00:15:31,521
Making friends
wasn't a priority for her.

163
00:15:32,031 --> 00:15:33,294
I hear she didn't date much.

164
00:15:33,766 --> 00:15:35,701
- Who told you that?
- Her dad.

165
00:15:35,868 --> 00:15:38,303
Oh, she'd never tell her father.

166
00:15:38,971 --> 00:15:42,169
But the way she acted sometimes,

167
00:15:42,375 --> 00:15:45,140
you know, the
things she'd let slip,

168
00:15:46,279 --> 00:15:48,009
I think there was a guy.

169
00:15:48,714 --> 00:15:50,580
She just never talked about him.

170
00:16:46,739 --> 00:16:48,435
How did you know it was me?

171
00:16:48,608 --> 00:16:49,701
Your handwriting.

172
00:16:49,876 --> 00:16:54,541
It took me a while, but I matched it
with a sample from the faculty files.

173
00:16:55,781 --> 00:16:58,979
I don't make a habit of
having affairs with my students.

174
00:16:59,151 --> 00:17:01,017
She's the only one.

175
00:17:01,387 --> 00:17:03,652
She was very special.

176
00:17:03,823 --> 00:17:06,122
How long had it been going on?

177
00:17:06,292 --> 00:17:08,488
About six months.

178
00:17:08,895 --> 00:17:11,865
It was all my doing. She
was never comfortable with it.

179
00:17:12,031 --> 00:17:15,399
She was always terrified
that her father would find out,

180
00:17:15,568 --> 00:17:19,664
or my wife would find out,
or the school would find out.

181
00:17:20,740 --> 00:17:23,073
I was the one
that wouldn't let go.

182
00:17:23,242 --> 00:17:25,108
Weren't you afraid
someone would find out?

183
00:17:25,278 --> 00:17:27,110
I was in love with her.

184
00:17:28,581 --> 00:17:30,072
When was the last
time you saw her?

185
00:17:30,650 --> 00:17:32,778
At the competition.

186
00:17:33,252 --> 00:17:35,812
It was the best I
ever heard her play.

187
00:17:36,355 --> 00:17:38,950
She killed herself before
I had a chance to tell her.

188
00:17:40,426 --> 00:17:42,088
We don't think
she killed herself.

189
00:17:43,563 --> 00:17:44,622
You don't?

190
00:17:44,797 --> 00:17:46,026
No.

191
00:17:46,198 --> 00:17:47,689
Where were you the
morning she died?

192
00:17:50,803 --> 00:17:53,602
Every Friday morning, I'm
at the music hall downtown

193
00:17:53,773 --> 00:17:55,435
rehearsing with
the philharmonic.

194
00:18:00,413 --> 00:18:03,872
Here. Let me play it.

195
00:18:20,232 --> 00:18:22,167
It's beautiful.

196
00:18:24,136 --> 00:18:26,605
She meant a great deal to me.

197
00:18:28,274 --> 00:18:32,439
I was just an older man trying
to make her fall in love with me.

198
00:18:38,084 --> 00:18:40,519
Oh, God, it's Sandra.

199
00:18:44,523 --> 00:18:47,015
Please. She didn't know.

200
00:18:55,134 --> 00:18:58,536
Michael? I tried to get your shirts,
but the dry-cleaner screwed up.

201
00:18:58,704 --> 00:19:01,071
I'll have to go
back later... Oh.

202
00:19:01,540 --> 00:19:03,975
Sandra Baldwin, this
is Leanne Mclntyre.

203
00:19:04,143 --> 00:19:06,203
She's working on the
Juliet DeLong case.

204
00:19:06,379 --> 00:19:07,938
Hello.

205
00:19:08,114 --> 00:19:11,141
Frank DeLong has no one to
blame for this tragedy but himself.

206
00:19:11,684 --> 00:19:12,708
Excuse me?

207
00:19:12,885 --> 00:19:14,911
Pushing Juliet to
suicide like that.

208
00:19:15,087 --> 00:19:17,147
It was only a piano
competition, for God's sake.

209
00:19:18,157 --> 00:19:24,028
Sandra has very little patience
with stage mothers or stage fathers.

210
00:19:24,196 --> 00:19:26,961
I was just telling your
husband, Juliet didn't kill herself.

211
00:19:27,133 --> 00:19:29,068
At least, we don't think so.

212
00:19:30,002 --> 00:19:31,664
Well, I've taken up
enough of your time.

213
00:19:31,837 --> 00:19:34,807
Nice to meet you. Bye.

214
00:19:50,556 --> 00:19:52,650
They're here, Dad.

215
00:19:58,097 --> 00:19:59,190
Russ. RUSS: Ben.

216
00:19:59,365 --> 00:20:01,425
Russ. Russ. Come in here.

217
00:20:01,600 --> 00:20:03,796
- Oh, Russ. Yeah.
- Long time.

218
00:20:03,969 --> 00:20:06,234
- This is my daughter, Leanne.
- Oh, nice to meet you.

219
00:20:06,405 --> 00:20:08,237
- You too.
- Give me that hand.

220
00:20:08,441 --> 00:20:09,704
Ow! MATLOCK: Huh? Oh.

221
00:20:09,942 --> 00:20:12,070
- Oh, I'm sorry. I'm sorry.
- It's okay.

222
00:20:12,244 --> 00:20:15,078
Well, you're looking good, Russ.

223
00:20:15,247 --> 00:20:19,150
- I'm doing pretty good. You look fine.
- Oh, I'm just fair, fair.

224
00:20:19,318 --> 00:20:20,911
I was telling...

225
00:20:21,087 --> 00:20:23,113
I said, I was just
telling Leanne

226
00:20:23,289 --> 00:20:24,449
how much fun we used to have.

227
00:20:24,590 --> 00:20:26,286
- Yeah.
- Yeah. Yeah.

228
00:20:26,459 --> 00:20:29,793
Remember the time we knocked
over Old Man McCabe's outhouse,

229
00:20:29,962 --> 00:20:32,295
and Junior McCabe
was still in it?

230
00:20:36,035 --> 00:20:39,563
Well, in honor of all
the fun we used to have,

231
00:20:39,739 --> 00:20:45,178
we fixed your favorite
meal, venison stew. Heh-heh.

232
00:20:47,079 --> 00:20:49,310
I don't eat red meat anymore.

233
00:20:50,483 --> 00:20:51,678
Oh.

234
00:20:51,851 --> 00:20:54,411
Well, don't worry about
it. I'll eat around the meat.

235
00:20:54,587 --> 00:20:55,782
It'll be fine.

236
00:20:55,955 --> 00:20:57,150
Oh.

237
00:20:58,958 --> 00:21:01,189
- Shall we?
- Yeah.

238
00:21:03,395 --> 00:21:04,419
Some dinner party.

239
00:21:04,597 --> 00:21:06,557
Guest of honor's a
vegetarian, and you serve meat.

240
00:21:06,699 --> 00:21:08,895
- Why didn't you tell me?
- I didn't know.

241
00:21:09,135 --> 00:21:11,229
It'll be fine, Billy, it'll
be fine. It'll be fine.

242
00:21:18,177 --> 00:21:20,373
Is there something wrong
with your salad, Russ?

243
00:21:20,546 --> 00:21:22,811
Tomatoes give me hives.

244
00:21:26,752 --> 00:21:28,243
Good stew, Leanne.

245
00:21:28,754 --> 00:21:29,983
Dad made it.

246
00:21:32,925 --> 00:21:34,120
Kind of runny, though.

247
00:21:36,829 --> 00:21:38,161
Is this zucchini?

248
00:21:38,564 --> 00:21:40,897
Yeah, I always put
zucchini in stew.

249
00:21:44,403 --> 00:21:45,769
It's a vegetable.

250
00:21:45,938 --> 00:21:48,407
I get these little
sores in my mouth.

251
00:21:49,742 --> 00:21:51,540
- Where you going?
- Get a drink of water.

252
00:21:51,710 --> 00:21:56,375
- Oh, have some tea.
- Oh, caffeine makes my hands shaky.

253
00:21:57,917 --> 00:21:59,510
What business are you in, Russ?

254
00:21:59,685 --> 00:22:03,053
Oh, I sell commercial
vacuum cleaners.

255
00:22:03,355 --> 00:22:05,449
Actually, that's the
reason I'm in town.

256
00:22:05,624 --> 00:22:06,819
That's interesting.

257
00:22:06,992 --> 00:22:10,827
Oh, yeah, this new model
that we have, it's a doozy.

258
00:22:13,232 --> 00:22:14,776
- Russ?
- What the hell happened?

259
00:22:14,800 --> 00:22:17,479
- The leg on the chair broke.
- You're trying to kill my brother.

260
00:22:17,503 --> 00:22:19,529
- Oh, Billy.
- Let's just get him up.

261
00:22:19,705 --> 00:22:21,936
- You okay?
- Let's just get him up.

262
00:22:22,107 --> 00:22:24,440
Ooh! My back.

263
00:22:24,610 --> 00:22:28,672
I must have slipped a
disk when I hit the floor.

264
00:22:28,848 --> 00:22:33,286
Oh, don't worry about
it. I'll be fine. Ooh.

265
00:23:06,886 --> 00:23:09,253
That's the song your
husband wrote for Juliet.

266
00:23:09,955 --> 00:23:11,287
You knew about them.

267
00:23:17,863 --> 00:23:22,460
I came across a rough draft of
the song in his office one day.

268
00:23:23,402 --> 00:23:26,964
Then I heard Juliet
playing it before class.

269
00:23:28,474 --> 00:23:29,840
I didn't wanna believe it.

270
00:23:31,310 --> 00:23:33,211
Then I saw the way
he looked at her.

271
00:23:33,379 --> 00:23:37,248
Kind of the way he looked
at me a long time ago.

272
00:23:37,650 --> 00:23:42,850
And then I knew that she and
my husband were having an affair.

273
00:23:43,355 --> 00:23:47,122
- How long have you known?
- About two months.

274
00:23:47,293 --> 00:23:50,058
- And you never confronted Michael?
- No.

275
00:23:50,229 --> 00:23:52,789
I'm not an easy
person to live with.

276
00:23:52,965 --> 00:23:58,905
I'm opinionated,
blunt, selfish, impatient.

277
00:23:59,071 --> 00:24:02,769
I can see why Michael strayed.

278
00:24:02,942 --> 00:24:05,377
But he didn't leave me.

279
00:24:06,211 --> 00:24:09,909
I was afraid that if I confronted him,
he would feel obliged to move out.

280
00:24:10,082 --> 00:24:11,710
I didn't want that.

281
00:24:13,585 --> 00:24:15,019
You still love him.

282
00:24:16,422 --> 00:24:19,153
I'm very stubborn
too. Did I mention that?

283
00:24:19,325 --> 00:24:21,920
Michael said that he was
rehearsing with the philharmonic

284
00:24:22,094 --> 00:24:24,654
the morning that Juliet died. Do
you remember where you were?

285
00:24:24,830 --> 00:24:28,358
Where I've been all week,
right here, grading blue books.

286
00:24:28,534 --> 00:24:30,093
The Conservatory
Chopin Competition

287
00:24:30,269 --> 00:24:31,862
always wreaks havoc
with my schedule.

288
00:24:32,037 --> 00:24:34,939
Oh, speaking of which, I've
got to get over to the auditorium.

289
00:24:35,107 --> 00:24:37,235
The final round
starts in 15 minutes.

290
00:24:37,643 --> 00:24:39,009
Would you mind
if I tagged along?

291
00:24:39,178 --> 00:24:41,738
I've never been to a
piano competition before.

292
00:24:42,481 --> 00:24:43,505
Sure.

293
00:24:43,682 --> 00:24:47,312
But I may not be the
best person to sit with.

294
00:24:47,486 --> 00:24:49,216
I've never won one.

295
00:25:58,424 --> 00:25:59,915
Here we go.

296
00:26:02,094 --> 00:26:04,256
Oh, this is exciting. - Yes.

297
00:26:05,264 --> 00:26:09,395
And the winner of this
year's Chopin Competition is:

298
00:26:13,238 --> 00:26:14,262
Miss Rebecca Smith.

299
00:26:33,225 --> 00:26:35,490
- Do you believe that?
- It wouldn't have been my choice.

300
00:26:35,661 --> 00:26:37,596
- What's wrong?
- What's wrong? The girl stank.

301
00:26:37,763 --> 00:26:40,358
- She wasn't that bad.
- Oh, are you kidding?

302
00:26:40,532 --> 00:26:43,366
Her tempo was erratic, her
interpretation was boring.

303
00:26:43,535 --> 00:26:45,375
She gave one of the
weakest performances there.

304
00:26:45,537 --> 00:26:46,800
Did she really?

305
00:26:47,272 --> 00:26:49,764
This is a disgrace.

306
00:27:01,053 --> 00:27:05,081
Mr. Baldwin, Ben Matlock. My
daughter spoke to you yesterday.

307
00:27:05,257 --> 00:27:06,725
This is Officer Wilson.

308
00:27:06,892 --> 00:27:09,885
We have a warrant here, sir, which
allows us to search your garage

309
00:27:10,062 --> 00:27:12,429
and examine it and its
contents for any fingerprints.

310
00:27:13,065 --> 00:27:15,159
Fingerprints?

311
00:27:16,401 --> 00:27:17,425
I don't understand.

312
00:27:17,603 --> 00:27:19,663
Well, the police believe
that Juliet may have died

313
00:27:19,838 --> 00:27:23,673
of carbon-monoxide poisoning
in somebody else's garage

314
00:27:23,842 --> 00:27:27,074
and then moved to
her own garage later on.

315
00:27:27,446 --> 00:27:29,779
You suspect me?

316
00:27:31,650 --> 00:27:34,085
You've got it all wrong.

317
00:27:34,253 --> 00:27:37,280
- I loved her.
- Leanne told me.

318
00:27:37,456 --> 00:27:40,119
But since your wife
is now a suspect too...

319
00:27:40,292 --> 00:27:42,523
My wife?

320
00:27:44,363 --> 00:27:48,300
Why is she a sus...? No.

321
00:27:50,169 --> 00:27:51,660
She didn't know about us.

322
00:27:52,905 --> 00:27:55,340
- Yes, she did.
- That's not possible.

323
00:27:57,209 --> 00:27:58,575
We'd like to get started, sir.

324
00:28:00,112 --> 00:28:03,173
Sir, could you open the garage?

325
00:28:03,949 --> 00:28:05,474
Sure.

326
00:28:12,958 --> 00:28:16,019
Ohh! Ben, is that you?

327
00:28:16,628 --> 00:28:18,187
Russ?

328
00:28:19,064 --> 00:28:22,831
You still here? I thought
Billy took you to the doctor.

329
00:28:23,001 --> 00:28:25,163
Oh, Billy came by,

330
00:28:25,337 --> 00:28:27,863
but when I tried to
stand up, I just couldn't.

331
00:28:28,040 --> 00:28:30,509
My back hurts so bad.

332
00:28:30,676 --> 00:28:32,668
Russ, what happened?

333
00:28:32,844 --> 00:28:35,439
Is it...? Is it age?

334
00:28:35,614 --> 00:28:39,346
You used to be so... and funny.

335
00:28:39,518 --> 00:28:41,987
Ben, that was high school.

336
00:28:42,154 --> 00:28:45,147
When I entered the
world of vacuum cleaners,

337
00:28:45,324 --> 00:28:49,785
I realized I just had to
develop a more serious side.

338
00:28:49,962 --> 00:28:51,988
Oh.

339
00:28:53,565 --> 00:28:56,592
Maybe you ought to take
some aspirin for your back.

340
00:28:56,768 --> 00:28:58,634
Upsets my stomach.

341
00:28:58,837 --> 00:29:00,135
Oh.

342
00:29:00,706 --> 00:29:04,473
Oh, you know what? It
would be so nice, Ben,

343
00:29:04,643 --> 00:29:08,205
if I could have the
phone just a little closer

344
00:29:09,181 --> 00:29:13,448
so I could reschedule my
vacuum demonstrations.

345
00:29:22,561 --> 00:29:25,156
Oh, ha, ha. There
you go. It just reaches.

346
00:29:25,364 --> 00:29:26,559
Isn't that wonderful?

347
00:29:26,732 --> 00:29:31,363
Oh, would you mind if some
of the calls were long-distance?

348
00:29:32,604 --> 00:29:36,268
I forgot my credit
cards in my other pants,

349
00:29:36,441 --> 00:29:39,934
and calling collect is so tacky.

350
00:29:43,315 --> 00:29:44,943
Okay.

351
00:29:45,250 --> 00:29:49,483
Oh, I can't quite
reach my briefcase.

352
00:29:49,655 --> 00:29:52,022
I wonder if you could
move the coffee table

353
00:29:52,190 --> 00:29:54,523
just a little bit
closer to the couch.

354
00:29:54,726 --> 00:29:56,922
Mm-hm. Yeah. Oh.

355
00:29:59,298 --> 00:30:01,790
Oh, now... Now, watch your
back. Don't you throw it out.

356
00:30:01,967 --> 00:30:03,663
You'll have to buy
a second couch.

357
00:30:03,835 --> 00:30:05,804
- How's that?
- You'll have to buy another couch.

358
00:30:05,971 --> 00:30:07,735
I say, how's that
with the coffee table?

359
00:30:07,906 --> 00:30:10,899
Well, that's fine, it's fine.

360
00:30:12,177 --> 00:30:14,237
It's a little dark in here.

361
00:30:17,249 --> 00:30:20,242
I wonder if you
could turn a light on.

362
00:30:22,587 --> 00:30:24,749
Okay.

363
00:30:52,317 --> 00:30:53,979
Hi.

364
00:30:54,886 --> 00:30:55,910
Hi.

365
00:30:56,722 --> 00:30:59,123
Heh. What are you doing?

366
00:31:01,093 --> 00:31:03,085
Um...

367
00:31:05,464 --> 00:31:07,262
Trying to figure something out.

368
00:31:10,635 --> 00:31:13,104
- What?
- You.

369
00:31:14,606 --> 00:31:17,974
I thought you'd given up
on that a long time ago.

370
00:31:18,777 --> 00:31:21,212
You knew about Juliet?

371
00:31:22,881 --> 00:31:24,577
Why didn't you say anything?

372
00:31:26,318 --> 00:31:29,379
Because I didn't know
what would happen.

373
00:31:31,923 --> 00:31:34,552
What if you denied it?

374
00:31:35,894 --> 00:31:38,523
What if you didn't deny it?
What if you asked for a divorce?

375
00:31:38,964 --> 00:31:40,626
Didn't you want a divorce?

376
00:31:41,433 --> 00:31:42,457
No.

377
00:31:49,975 --> 00:31:55,573
I thought if I didn't say
anything, if I didn't rock the boat,

378
00:31:55,747 --> 00:31:58,683
that somehow,

379
00:31:58,850 --> 00:32:04,414
maybe I could figure
out how to win you back.

380
00:32:12,464 --> 00:32:15,161
Well, that's very interesting.

381
00:32:18,203 --> 00:32:22,106
Because I've been sitting
here for the last three hours

382
00:32:22,274 --> 00:32:24,766
trying to figure out
how to win you back.

383
00:32:30,415 --> 00:32:32,941
Sandra, I'm sorry.

384
00:32:33,118 --> 00:32:35,178
I'm sorry.

385
00:32:46,431 --> 00:32:48,127
- Hi.
- Hi.

386
00:32:48,300 --> 00:32:51,668
Well, Michael and Sandra were doing
just what they said they were doing:

387
00:32:51,837 --> 00:32:53,567
philharmonic and blue books.

388
00:32:53,738 --> 00:32:56,207
- Yeah, they're not the killers.
- Yeah.

389
00:32:56,374 --> 00:32:57,899
How'd you know that?

390
00:32:58,076 --> 00:33:02,810
Because the police didn't find Juliet's
fingerprints anywhere in their garage.

391
00:33:02,981 --> 00:33:05,712
And you had me running all
over town all day for nothing?

392
00:33:05,884 --> 00:33:06,943
Why didn't you call me?

393
00:33:07,118 --> 00:33:10,850
Well, I've been too busy waiting
on Russ the Rhino in there.

394
00:33:11,022 --> 00:33:12,888
- Hush.
- Well, I have.

395
00:33:13,058 --> 00:33:15,186
I just got done fixing dinner.

396
00:33:15,360 --> 00:33:18,387
He wanted some chicken,
so I got him a bucket.

397
00:33:18,563 --> 00:33:20,122
It was too greasy,
he couldn't eat it,

398
00:33:20,298 --> 00:33:23,462
so I had to go to the store
and get some more and bake it.

399
00:33:23,635 --> 00:33:25,365
And I had to skin it first.

400
00:33:25,537 --> 00:33:28,336
- You ever heard of such a thing?
- Yeah.

401
00:33:28,507 --> 00:33:30,567
Had to skin it first.

402
00:33:30,742 --> 00:33:35,146
Maybe this is some kind of hateful
joke that Russ is playing on me.

403
00:33:35,313 --> 00:33:37,748
We used to play
hateful jokes on people.

404
00:33:37,916 --> 00:33:39,196
Maybe they're getting back at me

405
00:33:39,351 --> 00:33:42,116
by having Russ play
these hateful jokes on me.

406
00:33:42,287 --> 00:33:44,415
- Oh, don't talk that way.
- Well, he is.

407
00:33:44,589 --> 00:33:47,354
He's hateful, he's old,
he's mean, he's nasty,

408
00:33:47,526 --> 00:33:49,188
and he's hateful.

409
00:33:49,361 --> 00:33:50,921
Could be worse.
Could be Billy in there.

410
00:33:51,062 --> 00:33:52,894
I wish Billy was in here.

411
00:33:53,064 --> 00:33:56,364
I could yell at Billy. I could
just go ahead and kill him.

412
00:33:56,535 --> 00:34:00,734
Billy drives me crazy.
Russ makes me nuts.

413
00:34:00,972 --> 00:34:03,032
Would you...? Stop.
I'm... Just... I'll take over.

414
00:34:03,208 --> 00:34:05,143
- I got it.
- You're gonna break something. Dad.

415
00:34:05,310 --> 00:34:08,144
Fine. Fine.

416
00:34:08,747 --> 00:34:11,717
Well, I went to the finals
of the Chopin Competition

417
00:34:11,883 --> 00:34:13,818
at the conservatory
this afternoon.

418
00:34:13,985 --> 00:34:16,147
- Who won?
- Becky Smith.

419
00:34:16,321 --> 00:34:18,881
That the same girl that
beat out Juliet the first time?

420
00:34:19,057 --> 00:34:21,390
Yep. Frank DeLong said
she didn't deserve to win.

421
00:34:21,560 --> 00:34:22,800
Said she didn't play very well.

422
00:34:22,928 --> 00:34:26,228
Of course, I couldn't tell the difference.
She sounded like Van Cliburn to me.

423
00:34:28,166 --> 00:34:29,759
Um...

424
00:34:30,802 --> 00:34:32,327
Uh...

425
00:34:33,538 --> 00:34:35,734
I'm going out
for a little while.

426
00:34:36,141 --> 00:34:38,906
- Where you going?
- I'll tell you when I get back.

427
00:34:39,077 --> 00:34:42,844
- Ben?
- I'll be right there, Russ!

428
00:34:44,616 --> 00:34:46,016
<i>No. No.</i>

429
00:34:58,463 --> 00:35:03,163
Ha, ha, ha, ha.
Hi. Hi. Ben Matlock.

430
00:35:03,335 --> 00:35:07,796
I'm sorry I'm late. I had
trouble finding a place to park.

431
00:35:07,973 --> 00:35:10,135
There's a lot right
across the street.

432
00:35:10,775 --> 00:35:12,903
Yeah, but you have
to pay to park there.

433
00:35:13,078 --> 00:35:14,273
What's going on here?

434
00:35:14,446 --> 00:35:16,446
I have a plane to catch
first thing in the morning.

435
00:35:16,615 --> 00:35:19,141
I know you do. That's why I
wanted to talk to you tonight.

436
00:35:19,317 --> 00:35:21,479
I have a few questions

437
00:35:21,653 --> 00:35:25,488
about how you judge
these competitions.

438
00:35:25,657 --> 00:35:27,990
Like, yeah, well, for instance,

439
00:35:28,159 --> 00:35:31,857
do all three of you have to
agree on who's the winner?

440
00:35:32,030 --> 00:35:35,694
In this particular competition,
only two out of three are required.

441
00:35:35,867 --> 00:35:37,199
Is that how it was today,

442
00:35:37,369 --> 00:35:40,134
or did all three of you think
that Becky Smith was...?

443
00:35:40,305 --> 00:35:41,671
Oh, please.

444
00:35:41,840 --> 00:35:43,280
Let's not get into that again.

445
00:35:43,375 --> 00:35:45,537
I'm sorry, but a 10 year
old could have played

446
00:35:45,710 --> 00:35:47,804
as well as Miss Smith did today,

447
00:35:47,979 --> 00:35:51,746
and the sonata she played
Tuesday, she absolutely savaged.

448
00:35:51,916 --> 00:35:53,782
You just don't
like either piece.

449
00:35:53,952 --> 00:35:56,683
I don't like the way
she played them.

450
00:35:56,855 --> 00:36:00,451
Her fingering was awkward. She
had no sense of style or virtuosity.

451
00:36:00,625 --> 00:36:04,460
- Well, the others played worse.
- They played infinitely better.

452
00:36:04,629 --> 00:36:07,428
Becky Smith had no right
to win that competition.

453
00:36:07,599 --> 00:36:09,465
Well, the two of us
thought differently.

454
00:36:09,634 --> 00:36:12,160
Well, the two of you should
be looking for another job.

455
00:36:12,337 --> 00:36:14,137
- Now, you just wait a minute.
- How dare you?

456
00:36:14,272 --> 00:36:17,140
Thank you. Thank you. I just
wanted to find out how it worked,

457
00:36:17,308 --> 00:36:19,436
and now I think I know.

458
00:36:19,611 --> 00:36:22,672
So thank you for your
time, and good night.

459
00:36:22,847 --> 00:36:27,876
And have a... Have
a pleasant, safe trip.

460
00:36:28,053 --> 00:36:31,217
And again, thank
you. Good night.

461
00:36:39,431 --> 00:36:40,797
I think I know.

462
00:36:42,000 --> 00:36:45,937
It's pretty far-out,
but I think I know.

463
00:36:55,747 --> 00:36:57,875
Now, you see, what
you gotta know is that

464
00:36:58,049 --> 00:37:00,541
that works on all
kinds of carpets.

465
00:37:00,719 --> 00:37:05,214
It works on, oh, pile, loop,
everything, and it dries...

466
00:37:05,390 --> 00:37:07,291
It dries overnight.

467
00:37:07,459 --> 00:37:08,791
Now, you feel that?

468
00:37:08,960 --> 00:37:12,590
That's like pushing butter
on a hot skillet, isn't it?

469
00:37:12,764 --> 00:37:16,292
Now, a baby could operate that thing.
But let me show you something else.

470
00:37:16,468 --> 00:37:18,869
You see that can of
sawdust over there?

471
00:37:19,037 --> 00:37:22,530
Pick it up and dump it on the
floor. Just dump it right on the floor.

472
00:37:22,707 --> 00:37:24,903
Everywhere on the
floor. That's it. That's it.

473
00:37:25,076 --> 00:37:26,796
- Now, get it on there good.
- Are you sure?

474
00:37:26,945 --> 00:37:29,210
Now, you step on it.
Now grind it down good.

475
00:37:29,380 --> 00:37:31,747
- Good, good, good. That's perfect.
- Oh, grind it down?

476
00:37:31,916 --> 00:37:35,216
Okay. Okay. Now stand back,

477
00:37:35,386 --> 00:37:39,847
because this puppy's gonna
suck the daylight out of this carpet.

478
00:37:41,326 --> 00:37:43,522
Now, will you look at that?

479
00:37:43,695 --> 00:37:46,324
Will you look at that?

480
00:37:46,831 --> 00:37:51,235
Like a tornado over a
field of cotton, ain't it?

481
00:37:56,241 --> 00:37:57,675
I don't mean to be a tattletale,

482
00:37:57,842 --> 00:38:01,904
but Russ is grinding sawdust
into the living-room carpet.

483
00:38:02,080 --> 00:38:05,642
He's been dumping dirt and
grime, every kind known to man,

484
00:38:05,817 --> 00:38:07,979
in there since
9:00 this morning.

485
00:38:08,153 --> 00:38:11,123
And now the vacuum is
picking up all that dirt and grime.

486
00:38:11,289 --> 00:38:13,019
Well, that's good.

487
00:38:13,191 --> 00:38:15,751
And you know who is
operating the vacuum cleaner?

488
00:38:15,927 --> 00:38:19,056
You guessed it. Russ the Rhino.

489
00:38:19,230 --> 00:38:20,698
You're kidding.

490
00:38:20,865 --> 00:38:24,302
There's nothing wrong
with him or his back.

491
00:38:24,636 --> 00:38:25,763
He forgot himself.

492
00:38:25,937 --> 00:38:28,168
He had to be one with
the vacuum cleaner.

493
00:38:28,640 --> 00:38:32,338
He flew off that couch
like he was 17 years old

494
00:38:32,510 --> 00:38:35,878
on his way to knock down
Old Man McCabe's outhouse.

495
00:38:41,386 --> 00:38:43,252
It was a trick.

496
00:38:43,421 --> 00:38:49,588
The whole thing was a
mean, nasty, hateful trick.

497
00:38:54,199 --> 00:38:56,464
- Did you talk to Becky?
- Yup.

498
00:38:56,634 --> 00:38:57,693
She got an alibi?

499
00:38:57,869 --> 00:39:01,169
Mm-hm. She said she was giving a
piano lesson to this kid named Kyle

500
00:39:01,339 --> 00:39:03,171
over at her grandmother's house.

501
00:39:03,341 --> 00:39:04,621
When I spoke to her grandmother,

502
00:39:04,776 --> 00:39:06,938
she said she wasn't
sure that Becky was there,

503
00:39:07,111 --> 00:39:09,945
because she had been at the
senior center that whole day.

504
00:39:10,114 --> 00:39:11,582
She teaches aerobics.

505
00:39:12,884 --> 00:39:14,318
Did you talk to Kyle?

506
00:39:14,485 --> 00:39:16,078
Mm-hm. He said Becky
gave him a lesson,

507
00:39:16,254 --> 00:39:19,019
but he also said that right
in the middle of the lesson,

508
00:39:19,190 --> 00:39:22,058
she left for about 20 minutes,
said she had to make a phone call.

509
00:39:25,063 --> 00:39:27,658
How far is it between
Becky's grandmother's house

510
00:39:27,832 --> 00:39:29,391
and Frank DeLong's house?

511
00:39:30,635 --> 00:39:32,035
Less than a five-minute drive.

512
00:39:32,203 --> 00:39:34,638
- You clocked it?
- I clocked it.

513
00:39:55,326 --> 00:39:58,023
Miss Smith? Hi.

514
00:39:58,897 --> 00:40:02,493
You don't... You don't know me.

515
00:40:02,667 --> 00:40:04,727
You don't know me, but I
know a good bit about you.

516
00:40:04,903 --> 00:40:07,702
I'm Ben Matlock. Leanne
Mclntyre's my daughter.

517
00:40:08,439 --> 00:40:10,203
Haven't I answered
enough questions?

518
00:40:10,375 --> 00:40:12,344
Just a few more.

519
00:40:12,510 --> 00:40:14,911
I spoke to Dr. Wadell.

520
00:40:15,079 --> 00:40:16,741
- Who?
- Daphne Wadell.

521
00:40:16,915 --> 00:40:18,281
One of the judges you paid off

522
00:40:18,449 --> 00:40:20,418
to make sure you'd
win the competition.

523
00:40:20,852 --> 00:40:22,732
I don't know what the
hell you're talking about.

524
00:40:22,887 --> 00:40:25,914
Well, you didn't need to win that
competition for the scholarship,

525
00:40:26,090 --> 00:40:29,424
but you knew you'd never get
into the conservatory fair and square

526
00:40:29,594 --> 00:40:31,256
by, you know, just
good piano-playing,

527
00:40:31,429 --> 00:40:34,399
so you kind of bought
your way in, didn't you?

528
00:40:34,565 --> 00:40:40,197
I've heard of people being paid
off and bribed in sports and politics,

529
00:40:40,371 --> 00:40:44,706
but I've never heard of it in
the arts and classical music.

530
00:40:44,876 --> 00:40:48,870
If Dr. Wadell told you I
bribed her, she's lying.

531
00:40:49,047 --> 00:40:51,380
Oh, no, no, no. She
didn't say anything to me.

532
00:40:51,549 --> 00:40:55,611
Neither did Dr. Chomsky,
the other judge that you bribed.

533
00:40:55,787 --> 00:40:57,779
Then why did you come here?

534
00:40:57,956 --> 00:41:03,054
Uh, for the same
reason Juliet did.

535
00:41:03,928 --> 00:41:04,952
What do you mean?

536
00:41:06,364 --> 00:41:10,734
Well, Juliet probably
figured it out too

537
00:41:10,902 --> 00:41:14,304
and wanted to give you a
chance to disqualify yourself

538
00:41:14,472 --> 00:41:15,940
before she turned you in.

539
00:41:16,107 --> 00:41:19,168
I don't know. But one
thing I do know for sure,

540
00:41:19,344 --> 00:41:22,712
somehow you
drugged that poor girl

541
00:41:22,880 --> 00:41:25,372
and trapped her in your
grandmother's garage

542
00:41:25,550 --> 00:41:28,782
with the car running
and left her there to die.

543
00:41:28,953 --> 00:41:31,320
You knew your grandmother
would be gone all day

544
00:41:31,489 --> 00:41:32,889
and would never see her.

545
00:41:33,524 --> 00:41:36,858
At about 2:00, you drove
the body back to Juliet's house

546
00:41:37,028 --> 00:41:39,520
and staged it so it
would look like a suicide.

547
00:41:40,131 --> 00:41:42,896
Around 2:00, I was in the
middle of a piano lesson.

548
00:41:43,067 --> 00:41:44,865
Kyle Hinckley. I spoke to Kyle.

549
00:41:45,036 --> 00:41:47,801
He said that you were
gone for a good 20 minutes.

550
00:41:47,972 --> 00:41:51,238
That's more than enough time
to get to Juliet's house and back.

551
00:41:52,143 --> 00:41:56,979
Not only did I bribe two judges,
but now you're saying I killed Juliet?

552
00:41:58,816 --> 00:42:02,150
Juliet wasn't in your
grandmother's garage at all that day?

553
00:42:02,320 --> 00:42:03,720
Of course not.

554
00:42:03,888 --> 00:42:06,289
How about opening it
up? Let me take a look.

555
00:42:06,457 --> 00:42:08,983
Be just a second, and
I'll be gone, promise.

556
00:42:09,494 --> 00:42:11,326
Deal.

557
00:42:32,850 --> 00:42:36,946
What happened in here?
What's all that blue stuff?

558
00:42:37,121 --> 00:42:40,990
Police dusted for fingerprints.
Your grandmother let them in.

559
00:42:41,159 --> 00:42:44,926
So if they didn't find anything,
what are you doing here?

560
00:42:45,096 --> 00:42:46,860
How do you know they
didn't find anything?

561
00:42:47,031 --> 00:42:49,865
Because if they had,
they'd be here, not you.

562
00:42:50,034 --> 00:42:53,027
And of course, there
is nothing to find.

563
00:42:53,204 --> 00:42:57,005
Oh, you cleaned this
place to a fare-thee-well.

564
00:42:57,175 --> 00:43:01,169
Floors, walls,
doorknobs, everything.

565
00:43:01,345 --> 00:43:04,543
Took them close to an
hour to find any prints at all.

566
00:43:04,715 --> 00:43:07,776
But they didn't find
Juliet's, did they?

567
00:43:09,587 --> 00:43:12,352
Yes, they did.

568
00:43:13,257 --> 00:43:15,556
You missed a place.

569
00:43:16,761 --> 00:43:19,754
Either you didn't see it
because the door was up

570
00:43:19,931 --> 00:43:21,832
or you didn't think of it.

571
00:43:22,934 --> 00:43:24,368
See that spot right there?

572
00:43:24,902 --> 00:43:28,134
I guess the drugs you
gave her had worn off,

573
00:43:28,306 --> 00:43:31,140
and she woke up and
started trying to open this door.

574
00:43:31,309 --> 00:43:33,403
She yanked at it
with both hands,

575
00:43:33,578 --> 00:43:37,709
but she was trapped,
and the car was running.

576
00:43:37,882 --> 00:43:43,844
The police found six
real good, clear prints.

577
00:43:45,690 --> 00:43:46,885
Speak of the devil.

578
00:44:00,071 --> 00:44:01,869
Didn't finish your soup, Russ.

579
00:44:02,039 --> 00:44:03,940
I didn't eat the noodles.
Got egg in them.

580
00:44:04,108 --> 00:44:06,373
Oh, I'm sorry.

581
00:44:09,514 --> 00:44:12,211
- Russ the Rhino.
- In here, Ben.

582
00:44:12,383 --> 00:44:16,582
- Did you get the magazines?
- Oh, yeah. Models, see?

583
00:44:18,256 --> 00:44:21,283
<i>French Nites,</i> N-I-T-E-S.

584
00:44:21,459 --> 00:44:24,725
"Life as it really is on
the French Riviera."

585
00:44:24,896 --> 00:44:27,195
And his own private nurse.

586
00:44:27,365 --> 00:44:29,095
Oh, that's real
sweet of you, Ben.

587
00:44:29,267 --> 00:44:32,465
It'll be nice to have
someone look after him.

588
00:44:32,637 --> 00:44:34,697
Come in, Shirley.

589
00:44:34,872 --> 00:44:39,173
This is Shirley "Jawbone
Breaker" Hawthorne.

590
00:44:39,343 --> 00:44:41,471
Russ "the Rhino" Lewis
and his brother, Billy.

591
00:44:43,414 --> 00:44:45,076
Charmed, I'm sure.

592
00:44:45,850 --> 00:44:49,480
Her real expertise is
deep-tissue massage.

593
00:44:49,654 --> 00:44:53,056
According to the
woman at the agency,

594
00:44:53,224 --> 00:44:57,184
a couch-ridden person ought to get
one of these at least four times a day.

595
00:44:57,361 --> 00:44:58,795
Go to it, Shirley.

596
00:44:58,963 --> 00:45:01,432
Wait a minute. Wait, wait. Wait
a minute. Whoa, whoa, whoa!

597
00:45:01,632 --> 00:45:04,329
- No, no, no, wait, wait.
- That's right. Get in there.

598
00:45:04,502 --> 00:45:07,438
Oh, no. wait, wait,
wait. Please, wait...

599
00:45:11,175 --> 00:45:13,906
No, no, no. Please.

600
00:45:14,078 --> 00:45:16,638
Oh, my God. Oh, no, no.

601
00:45:16,814 --> 00:45:21,218
Will you wait,
please?! Oh, oh, oh, oh!

602
00:45:21,586 --> 00:45:23,748
Oh, ha, ha, ha!

603
00:45:24,222 --> 00:45:26,282
Actually, Ben, ha, ha,

604
00:45:27,024 --> 00:45:29,391
I think I'm starting
to feel a little better.

605
00:45:29,660 --> 00:45:30,923
Ha, ha, ha.

606
00:45:31,195 --> 00:45:39,195
I... I might even try to sit up.

607
00:45:42,006 --> 00:45:45,807
You know, I think,
as a matter of fact,

608
00:45:45,977 --> 00:45:50,540
I might be able
to... To... To...

609
00:45:52,817 --> 00:45:54,877
Look at that. He's
standing by himself.

610
00:45:56,587 --> 00:45:58,146
You know something?

611
00:45:58,322 --> 00:45:59,915
By the end of this day,

612
00:46:00,091 --> 00:46:03,118
I may even be able to
kind of walk out of here.

613
00:46:03,294 --> 00:46:07,197
Why wait till the end of the day?
Why not right now? Go to it, Shirley.

614
00:46:07,365 --> 00:46:09,664
No! No!

615
00:46:09,834 --> 00:46:12,201
Wait, wait. I'm well.
I'm well. I'm well.

616
00:46:12,370 --> 00:46:14,464
Hey, Russ. Hey, Russ.

617
00:46:16,240 --> 00:46:18,038
Gotcha.

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
